(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const updates={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-03":{"number":3,"title":"The Name Beneath the Bell","content":"The bell under Lanternwell Square was not supposed to ring.\n\nThat was what Neri, the valley's oldest resident, told us as we knelt beside the spring. Neri had grey curls, a walking stick cut from dark wood, and the unsettling habit of noticing questions before people asked them.\n\n“It hasn't sounded since the last lost traveler found their way,” they said.\n\n“Lost traveler?” I repeated.\n\nNeri looked at Maui. Maui suddenly became interested in examining a crack in the stone.\n\nThe crack had a sliver of blue-green moss growing along it. Maui prodded the edge with one finger.\n\n\"That stone is clearly losing an argument with the great Maui. I can settle it.\"\n\n\"Please don't,\" Neri said.\n\n\"It's a crack.\"\n\n\"It's the wall of a spring people drink from. Tamsin has been measuring it since the last hard rain.\"\n\nAt the edge of the square, Tamsin stood beside a bucket beneath a seep, checking two chalk marks. She called across that the crack hadn't widened and that she needed a date for the maintenance ledger, not a heroic demolition.\n\nMaui looked wounded. \"I've pulled entire islands out of the sea.\"\n\n\"Then fetching my ledger should be easy,\" Neri said.\n\nThe book was tiny. He carried it across the square as though it were a sacred mountain.\n\n\"Behold! An impossible feat of strength.\"\n\nNobody applauded except me. I laughed before I could stop myself. The sound helped; my key was still cold in my hand.\n\nThe spring had a life that continued whether I remembered anything or not: people tending it, people arguing over repairs, people arriving for water and leaving when they pleased. A place was not required to become a miracle just because I needed one.\n\nMaui set the ledger where it wouldn't get wet. \"If your name turns out to be magnificent, I claim partial credit.\"\n\n\"You haven't even heard it.\"\n\n\"Details. A proper legend leaves room for surprises.\"\n\n“The valley does not take people,” Neri explained. “Doors open. Travelers choose. Sometimes someone arrives before they remember the road behind them.”\n\n“Can the spring tell me who I was?”\n\n“It can show what is attached to the key. Whether you wish to look is yours.”\n\nI unwound the blue thread at my wrist. The brass key slid into my palm. There was a tiny notch near its teeth, shaped like a half-moon.\n\nNeri unfolded a small cloth beside the spring and set it across the stone.\n\n\"People often imagine a spring like this gives answers,\" they said. \"It doesn't speak in sentences. Sometimes it reflects something attached to the object you bring. Sometimes it shows nothing.\"\n\n\"What happens if I don't like what I see?\"\n\n\"Then we stop.\"\n\nMaui looked toward the water.\n\n\"Oh, good. A magical spring with rules,\" Maui said. \"I was afraid this place was going to be boring.\"\n\n\"Not always,\" Neri said. \"People can become desperate for an answer. I have seen them keep asking long after they were ready to listen.\"\n\nI touched the half-moon notch in the key.\n\n\"Would it hurt the spring to try?\"\n\n\"No. But it might hurt you to see something before you're ready. That is why I'm explaining it first.\"\n\nI thought about the way my mind had gone blank when I reached for a name. I had spent every night since the beach feeling that absence like a missing tooth. Part of me wanted to thrust the key into the water and demand that the whole world give me back whatever had been taken.\n\nAnother part wanted to keep walking and never ask again.\n\nNeri said, \"You could come back tomorrow.\"\n\nMaui shifted his lantern to his other hand.\n\n\"Or next week. The spring isn't going anywhere.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Pretty sure. It would be difficult to move this much stone.\"\n\nI laughed despite the tightness in my chest.\n\nI looked at the spring again.\n\n\"I want to try now,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded, then stepped back.\n\nMaui held the lantern while I approached the water. He did not reach for my hand. I was beginning to understand that his restraint was deliberate.\n\nThe moment the key touched the spring, a bell rang beneath the stones.\n\nI saw a kitchen with a window full of rain. A table covered in papers. Someone telling me I would always be the dependable one. The memory flashed away before I could decide whether the voice had been loving or demanding.\n\nThen came a word, clear as a pebble hitting the bottom of a well.\n\nJasper.\n\nI stumbled. Maui caught my elbow only after I reached for him.\n\n“That's my name,” I whispered.\n\nNeri waited.\n\nI said it again, testing the sound against the shape of myself. “Jasper.”\n\nMaui tried it with exaggerated solemnity. “Jasper. Excellent name. Strong. Dramatic. Goes with heroic companions.”\n\nThe little tattoo on his chest caught his eye, pantomimed a bow, and made a grand flourish toward me.\n\n\"Don't encourage him,\" I warned.\n\n\"Encourage me? This is restraint. I'm capable of commissioning a fifty-foot statue for a name like that.\"\n\n\"You are not commissioning a statue.\"\n\n\"Forty feet, then. Incredible negotiation skills.\"\n\nNeri made a sound that might have been a cough or a laugh. Maui bent dramatically at the waist, one broad hand across his chest. Then he straightened and said \"Jasper\" again, without an announcement surrounding it. His grin remained, but the word was mine and he seemed to know it.\n\nMini Maui pointed at the actual spring instead of the imaginary statue. That was probably the most useful advice either of them had given all morning.\n\n“I'm standing right here.”\n\n“Which proves my point.”\n\nI laughed, and the sound broke something open that fear had kept shut.\n\nFor a long time I stood there listening to the ordinary water.\n\nThe bell had fallen silent. People were going about their business beyond the fountain: someone lifting a basket of bread, a pair of neighbors discussing a leaking roof, a child laughing as a paper lantern turned in the breeze. Nobody stopped what they were doing to insist I explain what the spring had shown.\n\nI wondered how many of them had arrived by a door they didn't understand.\n\nNeri watched my face.\n\n\"Will you write the name in the arrival book?\" they asked.\n\nI hesitated.\n\n\"What happens if I do?\"\n\n\"It helps us address you correctly and return things you borrow. It doesn't mean you belong to the valley, and it doesn't give anyone authority over where you travel.\"\n\n\"Could I change it?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI looked at the book on the nearby table. There were empty lines between names, not the kind of narrow crowded spaces that made a person feel as though they had to squeeze themselves to fit.\n\n\"Then I want to write it myself.\"\n\nNeri handed me the pen.\n\nMy first attempt left a blot of ink beside the J. My hand shook, and I hated it for shaking. I almost reached for a fresh page.\n\n\"Can I leave the blot?\" I asked.\n\n\"It's your line.\"\n\nI wrote the rest.\n\nJasper.\n\nThe letters weren't elegant. They were mine.\n\nMaui leaned over the table.\n\n\"That looks very official.\"\n\n\"It's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Official handwriting.\"\n\nI nudged him with my elbow.\n\nNeri's registry had a second column I hadn't noticed. Beside the names were little marks showing whether a traveler wanted someone notified that they'd arrived.\n\n\"What does this symbol mean?\" I asked.\n\n\"It means the person chose not to have a message sent.\"\n\nMaui glanced at the page, then deliberately looked away when Neri moved it closer to me. He was trying; I could tell by the very serious expression he wore.\n\n\"Can I leave mine blank until I know who I might tell?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said. \"A name and an address are not the same thing.\"\n\nIt was a small distinction, but it loosened something in my chest. I had written Jasper without having to provide a history to justify it. I could decide later whether to contact the people behind the door, or whether to ask someone I trusted to help me do it.\n\nMaui pointed to the wet ink. \"What if you change your handwriting?\"\n\n\"Then my name won't fall off.\"\n\n\"Excellent. I had been concerned.\"\n\nI laughed. Neri slid the page beneath blotting paper and gave me a separate card for questions I wanted to ask before a future crossing. I wrote one: *Who can see my information?* The fact that I could ask that without being ungrateful for their help felt unexpectedly important.\n\nWhen we left, the bell remained quiet. My name didn't need to make it ring for the day to matter.\n\nHe grinned, then grew unexpectedly quiet.\n\n\"Would you rather I didn't tell anyone what happened?\" he asked.\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you might want to decide when people hear the story.\"\n\nI thought about it. The name was good news; the glimpse of the rainy kitchen was something I wasn't ready to unpack in front of strangers.\n\n\"You can tell them I remembered my name. Not the picture I saw.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Done.\"\n\nIt was a small agreement, made beside a fountain with somebody waiting to carry bread through the square. I had no idea yet how much I'd come to value it.\n\nI closed the arrival book gently and handed the pen back.\n\nThe spring held no more pictures. I expected disappointment, but I felt a strange tenderness toward the one small thing it had given me.\n\n“Does knowing your name help?” Maui asked as we crossed the square.\n\n“Yes. No. Maybe.” I looked at the houses. “I don't know if I liked who I was.”\n\nHe stopped near a row of lanterns.\n\n“Then don't decide that today.”\n\n“What if I never remember?”\n\n“Then you'll still be Jasper, if you want to be.”\n\nI stared at him. People who called themselves heroes were supposed to tell you what came next. Maui, who clearly adored the sound of his own voice, had somehow left the important space empty.\n\n“Is that all?” I asked.\n\n“Nope. Now I'm going to brag about how I helped the most fascinating person on this coast discover a name. It'll take at least twenty minutes.”\n\n“You didn't discover it. I did.”\n\nHe smiled, sharp and delighted.\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nThat evening I took the brass key to the little room Neri had offered above the Lanternwell bakery. I wrote my name on a scrap of paper and pinned it beside the bed, not because I feared forgetting it again but because I liked being able to choose where it belonged.\n\nDown below, Maui was telling someone a very inaccurate version of our morning.\n\nI opened the window.\n\n“You left out the part where you nearly fell in the spring!”\n\nHe looked up, grinning.\n\n“Artistic license!”\n\nFor the first time, the name Jasper felt less like a missing label and more like an introduction.","word_count":1822},"maui-12":{"number":12,"title":"A Place for the Departing","content":"The first person to ask whether they were allowed to leave the Lanternweald without saying goodbye was standing in my kitchen.\n\nThey were holding a loaf of bread in both hands. Mara had baked it that morning, and the crust had split along the top like a small mountain range. The traveler had bought it with two copper buttons and an offer to mend the bakery curtains.\n\n\"I can give the bread back,\" they said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"If it makes things easier.\"\n\nI stared at them. They stared at the floor.\n\nIt was the third time they had come to see me that week, and the first time they'd been able to ask the question.\n\n\"You can leave,\" I said. \"You don't owe anyone a reason.\"\n\nThe traveler's shoulders loosened. Not all the way.\n\n\"What about the register?\"\n\n\"Neri's arrival book?\"\n\nThey nodded.\n\n\"It's not supposed to be a leash.\"\n\n\"Everyone knows when I come in. Pip reads out the visitor names sometimes. I don't want anyone knowing which road I'm taking.\"\n\nI had assumed that a place full of doors would make leaving simple. I'd assumed many things since arriving here. My notebook was becoming a monument to the hazards of assuming.\n\n\"Can you wait until I check how the departure book works?\" I asked.\n\nThey hesitated.\n\n\"You don't have to wait,\" I corrected. \"I can meet you at the door later, if you want help. Or you can go now.\"\n\n\"Later,\" they said. \"And please don't tell the man with the hook.\"\n\nI found the public route map beneath a stack of bakery receipts. The safe path had Edda's turning measurements and Fen's tide window drawn in two colors. Nowhere did it require a traveler's name.\n\n\"Do you want this?\" I asked.\n\n\"Only if you don't tell anyone which door I'm taking.\"\n\nMara knocked on the open kitchen door. She had come to ask whether the traveler's offer to repair the curtains still stood. They froze.\n\n\"Only if you want the work,\" Mara told them. \"The bread was paid for. I can hire someone else to sew the curtains.\"\n\n\"I can't stay until tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then I'll ask someone else.\"\n\nNo complaint, no story about gratitude. Mara returned to her ovens. The curtains still needed mending; that didn't make the traveler responsible for them.\n\n\"I don't want to leave as somebody else's story,\" they whispered.\n\n\"You don't owe anybody the ending.\"\n\nI'd started writing their initial beside the route. I tore off the little corner and felt embarrassed by how automatic the motion had been. Keeping a person safe should not mean keeping them under observation.\n\nOutside, someone dragged something heavy across the cobbles. Maui's voice boomed about another heroic harbor achievement.\n\nThe traveler looked up, panicked.\n\nI folded the map with the directions facing inward and set it beside the bread. \"Take this when you're ready. I'll close the window.\"\n\nMaui chose that precise moment to come through the window.\n\nNot the door. The window.\n\nHe had a string of damp rope over one shoulder and sand in his hair. One foot landed on the kitchen stool. The stool protested so loudly that the traveler flinched.\n\n\"Good news!\" Maui announced. \"I secured the—\"\n\n\"Window,\" I said.\n\n\"Very efficient route.\"\n\n\"Door.\"\n\n\"Slower route.\"\n\nThe traveler went white.\n\nI set the bread on the table and stepped between them and the window.\n\n\"Maui, I need you outside for a minute.\"\n\nHe looked at my face and lost the grin.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nThat was progress. Last month he would have asked five questions and answered three of them himself.\n\nAfter he climbed back out, I walked the traveler to the covered passage behind the bakery. They gave me the name they'd chosen for the arrival book: Lark. They said it softly, like a name they weren't certain they were permitted to keep.\n\nWe agreed on a meeting place near the eastern arch at dusk. They did not tell me why they were leaving, and I did not ask.\n\nWhen I returned, Maui was standing outside with the injured expression of a man denied the chance to make a heroic speech.\n\nHe kicked at a pebble with enough force to send it across the courtyard.\n\n\"Great. I made a stranger afraid of me without even getting to do anything heroic. New personal record.\"\n\n\"Not every stranger needs the hero,\" I said.\n\n\"Some don't even want to meet him.\" Maui looked genuinely offended by that possibility, then caught my expression. \"Okay, fine. Most people do. A few have terrible taste.\"\n\nMini Maui sat on his chest, made an exaggerated sad face, then kicked an imaginary door shut. Maui covered the tattoo before it could complete the pantomime.\n\n\"I wasn't going to follow Lark,\" he said. \"I just wanted to know whether there was a reason they were scared.\"\n\n\"That's their story.\"\n\n\"I know the rule.\" Maui picked at the seam of his hook strap. \"I've wrestled monsters and argued with the sun. Apparently my newest weakness is not being everyone's favorite person.\" He gave me a sideways grin that didn't quite last. \"Terrible development for my reputation.\"\n\nThe honesty lasted a heartbeat. Then he straightened to his full height and announced that he was going to find something very heavy to lift while he contemplated his many admirable qualities. He chose a sack of flour Mara had already asked someone to move. That was about as healthy a response as I expected from him that day.\n\n\"Did I scare someone?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I didn't know they were there.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe folded his arms. \"If someone needs to leave, I can escort them. I know every safe path from the cove.\"\n\n\"They explicitly asked me not to tell you.\"\n\nHis expression sharpened.\n\n\"Do they think I would hurt them?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"Because that's their choice.\"\n\nHe looked toward the bakery. A child was chasing a dropped apple across the square. The world went on around his silence.\n\n\"You think I can't be trusted,\" he said.\n\n\"I think you want to be useful. That's not the same thing as being invited.\"\n\nMaui turned away.\n\nI let him have the space.\n\nBy afternoon I had a list of problems. Some were obvious: Pip sometimes announced arrival names before asking permission. The arrival book had a column labeled EXPECTED RETURN, as though every departure were a promise. Neri kept a spare key for every door but hadn't written down who could request one. The eastern arch had a railing that blocked one side of the approach.\n\nThe least obvious problem was that everyone wanted to help in their own preferred way. Which meant that a traveler who needed privacy had to reject kindness five times before getting what they'd asked for.\n\nNeri listened while I explained.\n\nThey rested both hands over their walking stick.\n\n\"The register was meant to protect travelers. If someone went missing, we wanted to know which way to search.\"\n\n\"Search only if they've asked to be searched for,\" I said.\n\nNeri's eyes dimmed. \"That is a difficult thing to say after you've lost someone.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\nI thought of the door to my old life. All the people I couldn't name, some of whom might be looking for me. I thought of how sick I felt when someone assumed not remembering meant I belonged to whoever recognized me first.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"More than I'd like.\"\n\nNeri looked toward the arches.\n\n\"We used to close them in storms.\"\n\n\"Did the traveler agree?\"\n\nThey did not answer.\n\nThe silence bothered me enough to write it down.\n\nAt dusk I met Lark beside the eastern arch. The air smelled of wet stone and burned rosemary. They had one travel pack and the loaf of bread wrapped in cloth.\n\n\"Are you coming with me?\" they asked.\n\n\"Only as far as you want.\"\n\n\"Can you stand back while I open it?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went to the railing and stood facing the square. Behind me a brass latch clicked. Cold air touched my neck.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI turned.\n\nLark was waiting in the doorway, their shoulders straight now.\n\n\"Thank you for not asking.\"\n\n\"Safe travels.\"\n\nThey smiled once and stepped through. The door closed.\n\nNothing terrible happened. No bell rang in protest. The lights at the well stayed lit.\n\nI found Maui at the cove checking the new tide chart. He had covered the little crab drawing with a square of parchment, then uncovered it again, as if even the crab's expression needed approval.\n\n\"They left,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"I saw the door light change.\" He paused. \"I stayed here.\"\n\nI leaned against the railing beside him.\n\n\"Was it hard?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI appreciated the answer.\n\nHe stared at the horizon. \"I've spent so much of my life proving I can get someone home. I never considered that getting out of the way might be the useful thing.\"\n\nI found his hand where it rested on the rail. He turned his palm up.\n\n\"I didn't tell you because Lark asked me not to,\" I said. \"Not because I don't trust you. Sometimes someone's privacy belongs to them even when keeping it makes us uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"I know that now.\" He glanced sideways. \"I might forget it.\"\n\n\"Then I'll remind you.\"\n\n\"You're very good at being annoying.\"\n\n\"I've been practicing.\"\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed my knuckles. The gesture was so unexpected that warmth climbed up my neck.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked, close enough that his voice lowered.\n\n\"You may.\"\n\nHe kissed me gently beside the new tide sign. The sea moved beneath us. He did not promise to be perfect, and I did not need him to.\n\nThe following morning, Pip and I rewrote the arrival register. There would be an optional contact and a private note indicating whether someone wanted assistance leaving. Nobody would read names aloud without permission. And where the old page said EXPECTED RETURN, we wrote one question instead:\n\nDO YOU WANT SOMEONE TO WAIT FOR YOU?\n\nLark's row remained blank.\n\nFor the first time, I understood that a blank line could be an act of care.","word_count":1688},"maui-18":{"number":18,"title":"One Door Closed by Choice","content":"The door to my old room was still there.\n\nI had avoided it for three days after the Lantern Walk, which was an impressive achievement considering it stood only a few turns from the square. It was an ordinary painted door set into a stretch of old stone. The Threadway did not advertise where it led. Only the small brass plate with my chosen name marked it as mine.\n\nMaui stopped beside me.\n\n\"Do you want me to come?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth. I could almost hear the magnificent speech he was about to give about his usefulness. Then he shut it and took one theatrical step aside. \"Fine. I shall guard this extremely dangerous patch of hallway against dust and suspicious furniture. You're welcome.\"\n\nI reached for the handle.\n\nThe brass was cold. When I turned it, a familiar smell met me: laundry soap, paper, and the faint dust of a room that had not been lived in for a while. The desk was where I remembered it. A book lay facedown beside a cup. The light came through the window at an angle I recognized without knowing why.\n\nThen I noticed the notebook.\n\nIt sat beneath the lamp, bound in faded green cloth, with a ribbon I could have tied myself. I knew the handwriting on the first page before I read the words.\n\nI had written a list of things I was afraid to ask for.\n\nThe first was simple: a day when I didn't have to explain myself.\n\nThe second had been crossed out so hard that the paper was nearly torn.\n\nThe third said: Don't let people decide where I belong just because they remember more than I do.\n\nI sat on the edge of the bed.\n\nI had expected the room to give me answers. Instead it gave me evidence of a person who had been frightened long before Lanternweald.\n\nFootsteps sounded in the hall behind me.\n\nMaui did not come through the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\" he called.\n\n\"I'm here.\"\n\n\"Are you all right?\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nThere was a long pause.\n\n\"Do you want me to wait?\"\n\n\"Yes. Outside.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI heard him move away from the doorway. Not far, but far enough to give me space.\n\nThat was harder than rescue. I knew it was harder for him, because it was hard for me too.\n\nI stayed in the room for another twenty minutes. I opened the desk drawer and found a broken pen, two bus tickets, and a photograph turned facedown. I did not turn it over. I wasn't ready to look at a face I might not recognize.\n\nOne ticket was creased nearly through the printed stop. The dates were different. I tried to remember whether either journey had been exciting or miserable, and my mind gave me nothing.\n\nThe silence was worse than a bad memory in some ways. There was no person to argue with. Just a scrap of paper proving I'd been somewhere without telling me why.\n\nMy fingers hovered over the photograph.\n\n\"Not today,\" I said aloud.\n\nI was talking to furniture now. Wonderful. I asked the desk whether it objected to the drawer remaining closed. It offered no opinion.\n\nA thud came through the wall, followed by a muttered complaint. Maui had almost certainly collided with something. I smiled before I meant to.\n\nI could love the man waiting outside without handing him every missing part of myself. I could keep the photograph facedown and still choose to return another day.\n\nOne of the drawer handles was loose. I wrote it in the notebook as a thing to repair when I was ready. Maui would undoubtedly offer to carry the entire desk away and call it a rescue. I'd tell him no. He'd make a scene. And then, if he was learning what I thought he was learning, he'd let me find a screwdriver.\n\nFor the first time since opening the room, the thought of tomorrow didn't feel entirely like a threat.\n\nInstead I wrote the date in the notebook.\n\nI live somewhere else now.\n\nThe words looked absurdly small.\n\nI added: That does not mean this room has stopped being mine.\n\nWhen I finally stepped into the hall, Maui was sitting on the floor with his back against the wall. He had taken the opportunity to carve a tiny fish into the handle of his walking stick.\n\nThe wooden fish was magnificent only by Maui's standards: one eye sat higher than the other, and its tail looked like a badly folded map.\n\n\"I was going to turn into a gull and perform a dramatic patrol of the roof,\" he admitted. \"Then I remembered you said you wanted time alone. Very inconsiderate of you, frankly. Do you know how entertaining a gull patrol would have been?\"\n\n\"Was that hard?\"\n\n\"Unbelievably. I have a whole hook and nothing to do with it.\"\n\nMini Maui popped onto his shoulder and gave him a congratulatory pat, then fell over laughing at the fish carving.\n\nMaui glared at his own skin. \"You try carving without a proper knife.\"\n\nHe wanted to ask me everything about the room. The questions crowded his face. Instead he held up the lopsided fish.\n\n\"Present for the heroic survivor of a very boring afternoon.\"\n\nI took it. He looked so pleased that I nearly forgot how frightened I'd been behind the door. Nearly. The fear was still mine, and he was waiting to see what I would share. He'd earned that place beside me by not turning a private crossing into a show.\n\n\"You waited,\" I said.\n\n\"You asked.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd get bored.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\nHe held up the fish.\n\n\"I gave boredom a face.\"\n\nIt was a much better fish than the one on the broom handle.\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen I cried.\n\nThe tears surprised both of us. Maui stood, but he didn't reach for me until I stepped into his arms. His coat smelled of sea air and the smoke from Mara's bakery. I pressed my face against his shoulder and let the feelings come without arranging them into a story.\n\nAfter a while he asked, \"Do you want to go back in?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you want to close the door?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI turned, took the handle, and pulled it shut.\n\nThe latch clicked. Nothing in the arch dimmed. No bell sounded in protest. The Threadway did not punish me for leaving a room I wasn't ready to inhabit.\n\nI stood there for a moment, breathing.\n\n\"Is this running away?\" I asked.\n\nMaui considered the door.\n\n\"I don't think so.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you know where it is. And you're deciding when to return.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That was a surprisingly good answer.\"\n\nHe grinned faintly.\n\n\"I'll try not to make a habit of it.\"\n\nWe walked back toward Tideglass in silence. Lanternwell Square smelled of bread and damp leaves. The frost was melting from the roofs. I noticed that the lantern I'd painted was still hanging beside the signal post, its wick extinguished and the circle of amber paint intact.\n\nBefore we reached home, I turned back toward the lantern by the signal post. The glass was misted from the cold. Somebody had replaced the wick while I was away, and the flame inside was steadier than the one I'd left.\n\n\"Who fixed it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Mara mentioned a volunteer on the morning shift,\" Maui said. \"They wrote it in the little service book.\"\n\nI found the book beneath the sheltered ledge. My earlier repair was still recorded there, along with the name of the person who had checked it today. The place didn't stop working when I stepped through a door.\n\n\"That's a strange thing to be relieved about,\" I said.\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"I suppose I thought everybody would be waiting for me to tell them what happened.\"\n\nMaui looked genuinely puzzled. \"We wanted you safe. That's not the same as wanting a report.\"\n\nThe words struck harder than I expected. I had a green notebook filled with memories of being useful, and somewhere in it a list I'd been afraid to show anyone. I didn't want those old obligations to walk out of the room with me and start issuing new orders.\n\nAt the house, I set my boots by the stove and looked at the fish carving Maui had left there. Its tail was uneven. He'd spent the afternoon sanding it rather than trying to follow me through the doorway.\n\n\"Can we have dinner before we talk?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And if I only tell you half of it?\"\n\n\"Then I'll know half.\"\n\n\"You're becoming dangerously sensible.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n\nHe set out bread and warmed soup while I changed into dry clothes. Nothing about the food was magnificent, but I found myself hungry for the first time all day. When I returned, he had pulled out the chair I liked without making a ceremony of it.\n\nI took his hand briefly before we ate.\n\nI didn't owe him every word in the green notebook to be worthy of his company. I could choose what to share and still choose to stay.\n\nAt home, I put the green notebook on the kitchen table.\n\nMaui set down the fish carving beside it.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what you found?\" he asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"A list I wrote before I came here. Things I was afraid to ask for.\"\n\nHe sat opposite me.\n\n\"What did you ask for?\"\n\n\"Mostly nothing.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"That's not very you.\"\n\n\"It was me. Maybe it still is, sometimes.\"\n\nHe thought about that.\n\n\"I've been trying to learn how to ask instead of deciding for people,\" he said. \"Maybe you're learning the same thing from the other side.\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nI turned the page and wrote a new list. Not demands. Wishes.\n\nI want to visit the old room again when I choose.\n\nI want Maui to stay tonight.\n\nI want room for other people I love without making anyone a substitute.\n\nI want to know what the bell means, but not at the cost of my right to say no.\n\nI read the list aloud.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nWhen I finished, he said, \"I can say yes to one of those right now.\"\n\n\"Which?\"\n\n\"Staying.\"\n\nI felt heat rise in my face.\n\n\"You don't have to be so pleased with yourself.\"\n\n\"Impossible.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand across the table.\n\n\"Do you want company because you're sad,\" he asked, \"or because you want me?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Both. Is that allowed?\"\n\nHe smiled, but there was no triumph in it.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went around the table and sat beside him. He kissed me softly, then paused when I caught his collar and drew him closer. The room was warm from the little fire. Outside, the sea moved in steady breaths along the stones.\n\nWe talked for another hour, about the old notebook and the strange relief of leaving something unfinished. We laughed about his carved fish, which had a suspicious resemblance to Fen.\n\nWhen I told him I wanted to go upstairs, he asked once more whether I was sure.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe left the notebook on the table and closed the bedroom door. The rest of the night belonged to us, with no demand to make grief beautiful.\n\nIn the morning, the notebook was still there beside the little wooden fish.\n\nI opened to the page I'd written.\n\nUnder the list, Maui had added a sentence in enormous handwriting.\n\nYOU CAN ASK ME AGAIN TOMORROW.\n\nI laughed so hard I nearly spilled my tea.\n\nThen I wrote beneath it, in much smaller letters:\n\nI probably will.","word_count":1963},"maui-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Visitor's Right to Leave","content":"The boy was sitting on his suitcase when I found him.\n\nHe couldn't have been more than nineteen, though his boots were worn in the way boots get when someone has been walking without much choice about where. His name was Tomas. He'd arrived through the northern Threadway three days ago and spent most of that time helping at the pantry. Now he was waiting on the steps beneath the arch.\n\nBeside him stood a woman named Vel, who had started calling Maui the Guardian of Tideglass after seeing him catch a boat during the mooring accident.\n\n\"You don't understand,\" Vel was telling him. \"People made room for you here. You can't just leave after they've helped.\"\n\nTomas stared at his shoes.\n\n\"I need to go home.\"\n\n\"Then why come at all?\"\n\nI stopped close enough to be heard without coming between them.\n\n\"Because people are allowed to visit,\" I said.\n\nVel turned toward me.\n\n\"The community fed him.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And gave him a bed.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And now he just walks out?\"\n\nI looked at Tomas.\n\n\"Do you want help reaching your door?\"\n\nHe nodded without looking up.\n\n\"All right. I'll check the safe route.\"\n\nVel put a hand on the suitcase handle.\n\n\"Someone should tell Maui.\"\n\nI felt my shoulders tighten.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"He's the one who welcomed the newcomers. He'll understand.\"\n\nTomas pulled his suitcase back.\n\n\"Please don't make me explain to him.\"\n\nIt was barely louder than the sea.\n\nI took a breath. Then I asked Vel to release the handle. She did, reluctantly.\n\n\"Neri has a private departure service,\" I said. \"No one has to explain their reasons to a crowd.\"\n\nVel looked hurt.\n\n\"I thought this was a place where people cared.\"\n\n\"It is. Caring doesn't make the doors ours.\"\n\nI escorted Tomas to the notice board. The departure instructions were clear, but somebody had hung a festival banner across half the text. I moved it aside. Neri was on duty and listened as Tomas asked for a route without giving an address.\n\n\"Would you like a guide to the arch?\" they asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Would you like us to keep your destination private?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nMaui arrived before we left, striding down the steps with his hook over one shoulder as though the ferry departure were an adventure he'd been promised. He saw Tomas's packed suitcase, and the grin he had brought for the crowd faltered. \"Okay,\" he said, turning the hook over in his hands. \"Tell me which part of this requires an enormous hero. I may need the answer slowly.\"\n\nVel straightened when she saw him. I saw the moment he recognized that look: pure, uncomplicated admiration, the kind he'd chased across oceans.\n\n\"Guardian of Tideglass,\" she said. \"Tell him he can't just take everything and go.\"\n\nMaui drew himself to full height. Mini Maui struck a triumphant pose over his heart. For half a second the temptation was visible. A grand speech, a grateful crowd, an audience that knew his name.\n\nThen he saw Tomas's hands clenched around the suitcase.\n\n\"Whoa,\" he said. \"Hold on. I rescue people. I don't collect them.\"\n\nVel frowned. \"But you made this place safe.\"\n\n\"With about a hundred other people, and several very angry clipboards.\" He grinned, then let the grin fade. \"That doesn't give me a vote on where Tomas goes next.\"\n\nMini Maui lowered his arms and looked mildly disappointed. Maui looked more disappointed still. He'd wanted to be somebody's guardian; being the person who opened a path without knowing whether he'd see them again was harder.\n\nWhen Tomas thanked him, Maui made a spectacular show of accepting the compliment. He didn't ask for a promise in return.\n\nNot because I'd summoned him. Vel had.\n\nHe came down the square with his hook over one shoulder and a confused expression.\n\n\"What's happening?\"\n\nVel stepped toward him.\n\n\"Tell him he should stay. We welcomed him.\"\n\nMaui looked at the suitcase, then at Tomas, then at me.\n\nI didn't rescue him from the choice.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said, \"do you want to go home?\"\n\nTomas nodded.\n\n\"Then do you need anything for the journey?\"\n\nVel stared.\n\n\"But the pantry—\"\n\n\"The pantry helped because he needed help,\" Maui said. \"Not because we bought another member of the village.\"\n\nHe said it plainly. No thunder. No boast.\n\nVel looked at him like he'd broken something she loved.\n\n\"I thought you understood loyalty.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then why aren't you asking him to stay?\"\n\n\"Because I wouldn't want someone to keep me somewhere by calling it gratitude.\"\n\nTomas looked up for the first time.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he whispered.\n\nWe walked him to the arch along the safe route. Neri came too, carrying the travel map. The Threadway glowed faintly in the stone, not bright enough for a spectacle. Tomas put his suitcase down, checked that he had his papers, and asked whether he could come back someday.\n\nBefore the final bend, Edda's temporary marker showed where a cart had scraped the bridge railing. A painted arrow pointed away from the damaged edge.\n\nMaui reached for Tomas's suitcase.\n\nTomas held on.\n\n\"Sorry,\" Maui said, drawing back. \"I was going to perform an astonishingly efficient carry.\"\n\n\"I know. I want to carry it myself.\"\n\nMaui turned to me. \"This village has conspired against my best tricks.\"\n\n\"Only the ones involving somebody else's luggage.\"\n\nHe groaned, then stepped onto the clear side of the path and made a sweeping bow. \"Your route, honored traveler. Free scenery. Unbelievably handsome guide. No obligation to tip.\"\n\nTomas smiled without surrendering the handle.\n\nNeri checked the printed map and found that it still showed the old, wider crossing. They penciled the correction and left a note for Edda.\n\n\"Does every path come with paperwork?\" Maui demanded.\n\n\"Only the paths people use,\" Neri said.\n\n\"Then I'll never be able to retire.\"\n\nWe waited while Tomas crossed at his own pace. The arch would not vanish because no one rushed him. Maui followed beside me with his hands conspicuously empty.\n\nWhen Tomas paused to check his documents, Maui looked toward the door and then deliberately looked away, giving him the privacy of a man who had traveled far enough to know his own destination.\n\n\"If you want,\" Neri said.\n\nHe looked at Maui.\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"We'll still have terrible rope and decent bread.\"\n\nTomas laughed.\n\nThen he stepped through.\n\nThe light settled. The arch remained open for other travelers.\n\nVel had followed us at a distance. When Tomas disappeared, she turned away without speaking.\n\nAfter Tomas left, Neri took the public arrival register back to the office. They didn't cross out his name as though he'd never been there. They marked the departure as confirmed, with no destination printed for curious strangers.\n\nA small girl came up to the notice and asked whether Tomas had gone away because he didn't like the village.\n\n\"Maybe he missed home,\" I said. \"He was allowed to go even if he liked us very much.\"\n\nShe considered that. \"My grandmother goes home after tea.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Nobody gets mad at her.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nThe girl ran off toward the bakery. I wished I could explain the matter as simply to the adults.\n\nMaui watched Neri lock the restricted ledger.\n\n\"I almost told Tomas he should stay until the next feast,\" he said.\n\n\"Would that have made him happier?\"\n\n\"I don't know. It would have made me feel better.\"\n\nThat was a difficult admission. I took his hand while we walked toward the river path.\n\n\"You're allowed to miss someone after they leave,\" I said. \"You don't get to make the missing into a duty they owe you.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb across my knuckles. \"Does that apply when you travel to see other people you love?\"\n\n\"Yes. I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. That doesn't mean I'll never be sad when we part. It means we can be honest without turning the sadness into a chain.\"\n\n\"I might need to be reminded.\"\n\n\"You can ask.\"\n\nWe passed Vel beside the market. She was still angry, and I was still angry about what she'd said to Tomas. I didn't approach her to demand an apology on his behalf. She had a right to understand her part in it at her own pace, even if I wished she'd start immediately.\n\nAt home, Maui put the returned carving on the shelf. I watched him choose not to throw it away.\n\n\"What if Tomas never comes back?\" he asked.\n\n\"Then the carving can still remind you that you cared.\"\n\nHe nodded. The little fish sat crookedly against the wall, plainly unfinished. Neither of us turned it around to make it look perfect.\n\nMaui watched her go.\n\n\"That hurt her,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I wanted her to understand.\"\n\n\"She might. Later. Or she might not.\"\n\nHe stared at the stones.\n\n\"I used to think having people admire me meant I had done something good. Now I know I can do something good and lose their admiration anyway.\"\n\nI touched his hand.\n\n\"That isn't a comfortable lesson.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nWe returned to Tideglass at dusk. Fen was setting up the tide chart for the next morning, and the first lanterns were flickering along the ridge. Maui offered to help carry the stand. Fen accepted without ceremony.\n\nFor a while that was enough.\n\nThe next day Vel arrived at our door with the little wooden fish Maui had once given her. She held it out.\n\n\"I don't want this anymore,\" she said.\n\nMaui took it.\n\n\"I'm sorry it upset you.\"\n\n\"It didn't upset me. It made me think you were someone who would always protect us.\"\n\n\"I will help when I can.\"\n\n\"That isn't the same.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said.\n\nShe left.\n\nHe turned the fish over in his hand. It was badly carved, with one eye much larger than the other. I remembered him making it on a rainy afternoon and declaring it a masterpiece.\n\n\"Want me to tell you she was wrong?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"Not right now.\"\n\nWe sat on the steps.\n\n\"I liked being the person she thought I was,\" he admitted. \"Even though he didn't exist.\"\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"People can be disappointed when you don't belong to their story.\"\n\n\"Even you?\"\n\n\"Especially me. That's why I have to keep asking what you want instead of deciding what your legend means.\"\n\nHe put the fish beside the door.\n\n\"Do you still want me?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even without a crowd?\"\n\n\"Preferably without a crowd.\"\n\nHe laughed, the sound small and tired.\n\nI kissed him, gently, then pulled back when he seemed to need room to breathe.\n\nThat evening he put the returned fish on the shelf beside the green notebook from my old room. Two objects from people who had wanted different things. Neither one had to be thrown away to make the lesson disappear.\n\nIn the morning I checked the departure notice.\n\nThe festival banner had been moved to another wall.\n\nSomeone had written beneath the instructions, in ordinary dark ink:\n\nLeaving is not a debt.\n\nI didn't know who had written it.\n\nI was glad someone had.","word_count":1862},"maui-36":{"number":36,"title":"Permission for Privacy","content":"The chalkboard in Lanternwell Square had acquired a new column.\n\nIt stood beside the bakery menu under the heading *Who's with Jasper?* Someone had drawn six little hearts and written the names of people I cared about, with blank spaces for dates and guesses.\n\nI stared until the letters stopped making sense.\n\nMara came out carrying a tray of rolls. She saw the board and went still.\n\n\"That's not mine.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I was going to wipe it off, but I thought you should decide whether you wanted to see it first.\"\n\nI swallowed. I was furious with the person who'd written it, but I was also strangely ashamed. The chalkboard made my life look like a game people could follow, score, and argue about over breakfast.\n\n\"Would you give me the cloth?\" I asked.\n\nMara handed it over.\n\nI wiped the names away.\n\nThe damp cloth left a grey-pink stain where the little hearts had been. It had also erased half the bakery's prices. A woman waiting for flour squinted at the board and asked how much a small loaf cost.\n\nMara looked at the missing figures. \"I'll put them back. You shouldn't have to redraw the whole market because somebody made a game out of your life.\"\n\n\"Could we have a separate board?\" I asked. \"For actual notices?\"\n\nPip was fixing a crooked lantern overhead. They climbed down to inspect the chalk marks.\n\n\"I can paint one,\" they said. \"But if strangers use my work as a rumor wall, I get to refuse to repaint it every morning for free.\"\n\nMara named a cost for materials from the market fund. Neri offered to put it on the next notice-board agenda. None of the work required explaining my relationships to a single shopper.\n\nMaui's expression sharpened. \"I could rip the old one off the wall. Problem solved.\"\n\n\"Then Mara has nowhere to list breakfast,\" I said.\n\n\"Right.\" He looked disappointed. \"Bad plan. I could guard the new one.\"\n\n\"From what?\"\n\n\"Terrible handwriting, obviously.\"\n\nPip pointed to his own enormous signature on a previous poster.\n\nMaui stared at it. \"That is magnificent handwriting.\"\n\nI laughed despite my anger. The square still needed bread prices, tide warnings and a way for people to ask for help. What it did not need was an audience for my private life.\n\nThe council could decide how the public board was used. I would decide whether, when and how I talked about the people I loved.\n\nBehind me someone said, \"Oh. I thought it was just a joke.\"\n\nI turned. A man I recognized from the market stood holding a basket of apples. He looked embarrassed rather than cruel.\n\n\"Whose joke?\" I asked.\n\n\"People were talking. You and Maui, and the others. We wondered how it all worked.\"\n\n\"Then you could have asked me a question that didn't require exposing six people's private lives.\"\n\nHe shifted the basket. \"I didn't think anyone would mind.\"\n\n\"You didn't ask them.\"\n\nI heard my voice grow sharper. I wanted to make him feel exactly as small as the board had made me feel. I was not proud of that.\n\nMaui arrived before I could say anything else. He looked at the damp board, then at my face.\n\nHis eyes followed the six hearts down the board. I watched the easy grin drain out of his face.\n\n\"Well,\" he said, very softly. \"Whoever drew this has a powerful imagination and absolutely terrible manners.\"\n\nMini Maui came running across his upper arm carrying an imaginary giant eraser. The real Maui looked ready to do the same thing with his hook and perhaps the entire board.\n\nI wanted that anger on my side. I didn't want him to turn it into a public spectacle that would make the story even harder to escape.\n\n\"Jasper,\" he said, voice suddenly big enough to make passersby turn, \"I could give a speech so magnificent they would never gossip again.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\nHe closed his mouth, clearly in pain.\n\n\"Fine. No speech. Extremely difficult, but fine.\"\n\nI saw the impulse hadn't gone anywhere. His fists were tight, and he was watching the man who'd drawn the board. But when I began to speak, he backed up and let people hear me. He wasn't calm. He was choosing not to make his anger more important than mine.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nI told him.\n\nHis jaw tightened. For a moment I saw the old Maui, ready to turn the whole square into a battlefield because someone had embarrassed a person he loved.\n\nHe looked at me. \"What do you want?\"\n\n\"Not a speech from you,\" I said quickly. \"Please.\"\n\nHe breathed in, then out. \"All right.\"\n\nI asked Neri for five minutes at the community notice board. A few people gathered, including several who had seen the chalkboard before it was erased. I kept the account plain.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. The people in my relationships know that. They each make their own choices, and some of those choices are private.\"\n\nA woman raised her hand. \"So you don't have to tell everyone?\"\n\n\"No. Neither do you.\"\n\n\"What about fairness?\"\n\n\"Fairness means honesty with the people affected, respecting consent, and keeping the commitments we actually make. It doesn't mean putting everyone's schedule on a public board.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether my partners liked each other.\n\n\"That's their business,\" I said. \"Some have worked together. Some want space. None of them owes the square a performance.\"\n\nThe man with the apples had stayed near the edge of the crowd. He looked down at his basket.\n\nI could have called him out by name.\n\nI didn't.\n\nInstead I asked the council to put a privacy rule beside the public festival guidelines: notice boards were for community business, not speculation about residents' romantic lives. Mara added a second rule about not writing personal contact information without permission.\n\nNeri approved the notice.\n\nIt wasn't a law against gossip. People would still talk. But it established a boundary the community could enforce in the spaces it maintained.\n\nLater, I found Maui at Tideglass repairing a lantern hook.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\n\"At the board.\"\n\nI leaned against the post. \"You didn't say much.\"\n\n\"You asked me not to.\"\n\n\"Was that difficult?\"\n\n\"Extremely. I had three speeches ready.\"\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nHe put down the hook.\n\n\"About the speech you heroically saved me from giving,\" Maui said. \"Does this mean if somebody asks what we do at home, I can tell them to mind their own coconuts?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I don't want to hear every detail about the others. Sometimes I do need to know when our plans change. And sometimes—\" He scowled at the hook. \"Sometimes my brain insists I should *win* at being loved. Which is ridiculous. And terrible for my winning record.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You can ask. I can decide what belongs to me to share. If it belongs to someone else too, I ask them first.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"What about kisses?\"\n\n\"Those are ours when they're between us. We can talk about them if we both want. They're not the village's property.\"\n\nHe smiled slowly.\n\nMara came back across the square with a fresh price board, still looking upset about what someone had written on the chalk.\n\n\"I was thinking,\" she said. \"Maybe the notice boards should be separated. People keep putting personal messages beside the public safety information because it's the only place everybody knows to look.\"\n\nNeri, who had been updating the departure instructions, stopped to listen.\n\n\"A public board for services,\" they said. \"A private message shelf for things addressed to one person. The shelf would need a way to refuse unwanted notes, too.\"\n\n\"And no list of who collects what,\" I added.\n\nMara nodded. \"I don't want the bakery becoming a gossip desk.\"\n\nWe worked out a small plan that afternoon. The service board would hold prices, closures, community meeting times and voluntarily credited work. Personal messages could be left in sealed envelopes through the registrar, who could return anything unwanted without posting it in public.\n\nPip offered to paint a sign. Edda made them check that the new shelf could be reached without blocking the walkway. A new rule wasn't finished just because someone had written it in attractive letters.\n\nMaui held the ladder while the sign went up.\n\n\"I could give a speech about everyone's freedom,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Would it be bad?\"\n\n\"Probably magnificent.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"And unnecessary.\"\n\nHe sighed theatrically, but let Pip fasten the last corner. When the square was tidy again, he reached for my hand and waited.\n\n\"I like that you can tell me what you want private,\" he said.\n\n\"Even when you want to know?\"\n\n\"Especially then. I may still be curious.\"\n\n\"I'll still tell you the things that affect us. You don't need a public scoreboard to know I care.\"\n\nWe stood beneath the new sign while people walked past without noticing us. It was wonderfully ordinary. The next time somebody wanted to announce who Jasper had kissed, the town at least had a clear reason to say that wasn't what the public board was for.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThe sunset lit the signal post in amber. I could hear Lio arguing with Fen about a skiff measurement and the bakery bell sounding faintly from the square. There was a whole life around us, full of people who deserved to have some parts remain their own.\n\nMaui stepped closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you somewhere nobody's taking notes?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe went home by the upper path, laughing when the wind tried to steal his cloak. At the door he kissed me, then again after I pulled him closer. The rest of the evening was private, the door closed because we had chosen it.\n\nBefore we left, Neri caught us beside the fountain.\n\n\"I have a problem,\" they said, carrying a second sheet of paper. \"We keep an arrival book so people can be found when crossings go wrong. If we stop taking names, we lose the safety record. But if we publish who went where—\"\n\n\"We do the chalkboard all over again,\" I finished.\n\nEdda, passing toward the archive, stopped to listen. She pointed out that the registrar's desk couldn't be reached without stairs after the side path flooded. That made an emergency contact fairly useless if the person who needed the record couldn't get inside.\n\nWe wrote a new rule together. The public board would show Threadway closures and available services, not visitors' destinations. A traveler could give Neri a restricted emergency contact, refuse to list an unrelated private appointment, and request correction without broadcasting their business. If someone challenged an entry, a second registrar would review it.\n\nMaui studied the page. \"That sounds like a lot of work just to stop people being nosy.\"\n\n\"It's work we should have done before the board hurt somebody,\" I said.\n\nHe didn't argue. Instead he held the ladder while Neri pinned the notice in a place Edda could actually read.\n\nThe man with the apples approached again.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he said. \"I made it a joke because I didn't think about the people underneath it.\"\n\nI was still angry. \"Don't do it again.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI wasn't required to make him feel better. He went back to his market stall, and I went home with Maui.\n\nOn the path, Maui touched my wrist. \"Ask about the ledger tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Ask about your other partners?\"\n\n\"Only what affects our agreements, like we said.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I am becoming a very sophisticated man.\"\n\n\"You held a ladder.\"\n\n\"Heroically.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek, which managed to shut him up for nearly ten seconds.\n\n\nThe next morning the chalkboard held the day's bread prices and the tide warning, nothing else.\n\nThere were six hearts still faintly visible beneath the damp surface.\n\nI didn't erase them again.\n\nThe boundary was the important part. The trace of somebody's mistake could stay without deciding what happened next.","word_count":2000},"maui-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Lantern Ledger","content":"The new ledger had no columns for loyalty.\n\nThat was Neri's first rule.\n\nWe sat at a long table in Moonroot Archive, comparing the surviving pages of the old arrival book with a clean blank volume. The old book had been designed for people who believed that crossing into Lanternweald created a debt. It recorded names, expected return dates, assigned keepers, and—in several places—marks that might have indicated whether someone had been permitted to leave.\n\nSome of those marks made my skin crawl.\n\n\"Are we keeping any of this?\" I asked.\n\nNeri touched the cover.\n\n\"We need to preserve the original as evidence. We do not need to make a new version of its authority.\"\n\nSeverus, working at the next desk, looked up.\n\n\"An excellent distinction.\"\n\nOrin had prepared a proposed format for the public record. It listed structural events, maintenance decisions, funding approvals, and testimony categories. Personal travel details would be kept only when someone requested help and only for as long as the service required. Romantic relationships were not part of the civic record.\n\nThe reading table had been lowered after Edda watched three visitors struggle to reach its writing surface. One leg was still propped on a wedge of scrap wood. Whenever Maui leaned forward, the ink bottle rattled.\n\n\"Stop attacking the furniture,\" Severus said.\n\n\"I'm reading,\" Maui replied.\n\n\"Then your technique requires revision.\"\n\nMaui stepped away with theatrical dignity. \"Observe. Flawless reading posture.\"\n\nOrin pressed the table's corner. It shifted anyway.\n\n\"The repair order is still open,\" they said. \"The record isn't public in any useful sense if half the people who need it can't write on the table.\"\n\nA charcoal line on the floor marked a turning space. Someone had moved the chair across it.\n\nI moved the chair back and fetched the order. One line specified the table leg, another a lamp that shone directly into a reader's eyes. Neither would reveal the erased keeper's name, but both mattered to the person who wanted to read the record.\n\nMaui raised his hook. \"I can hold the table steady all day.\"\n\n\"An admirable proposal,\" Severus said, \"if the archive intends to employ you as permanent furniture.\"\n\n\"Very handsome furniture.\"\n\n\"We have a maintenance budget,\" Orin said. \"We will use it.\"\n\nI noted the temporary assistance arrangement until the repair could be tested. Neri approved the sign. Severus returned to his evidence list. Maui put down his hook, apparently devastated that a perfectly good heroic job had been assigned to a carpenter.\n\n\"Nobody appreciates an architectural legend,\" he muttered.\n\n\"Nobody wants to write on one,\" I said.\n\nI wanted to hug the person who'd written that sentence.\n\nInstead I told Orin it was good work.\n\nThey looked delighted.\n\nThe hardest part was the blank line.\n\nIt belonged to the erased signature from the Closed Bells ledger. The investigation had documented the order, the materials, the closure, and Neri's witness account of a person denied departure. But the name on the original page was gone, and none of the surviving evidence established it with sufficient confidence.\n\nAvel, the councilor who had demanded a name before, came to review the draft.\n\n\"You're leaving the blank?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said.\n\n\"People will think you're protecting someone.\"\n\n\"Then the note beside it should explain exactly why it's blank,\" Severus answered.\n\nI turned the page around so Avel could read it.\n\n*Signature removed from the surviving document. Identity unverified. The harm caused by the closure is documented independently. Additional evidence may be submitted without mandatory public disclosure.*\n\nAvel read it twice.\n\nHe looked tired.\n\n\"I want someone to answer for what happened.\"\n\n\"So do I,\" I said. \"That doesn't give us permission to choose a person because the empty space makes us angry.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\nFor a moment I thought he might begin another argument. Instead he asked to see the evidence list.\n\nWe showed him the invoices, tide charts, witness summary, and the notes explaining what each could and could not prove. We also showed him the emergency policy changes already enacted because of the investigation: voluntary departure routes, clear access to the upper hall, no public lists of destinations, and reviewable decisions during closures.\n\nHe studied them.\n\n\"This is more than a blank,\" he said finally.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri answered. \"It is the work around it.\"\n\nBefore we released the draft, Orin caught a problem in the access rules.\n\n\"A public correction,\" they said, tracing the margin, \"might reproduce somebody's private statement if we're not careful. Suppose a person gives us an account in confidence and later asks us to correct the date. Do we announce the whole account to prove we fixed it?\"\n\nNeri looked at the page.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then the correction procedure needs two paths,\" Orin said. \"One for public evidence and one for confidential testimony.\"\n\nSeverus put down his pen.\n\n\"That is not a minor adjustment.\"\n\nOrin's face fell.\n\n\"It's a necessary one,\" he continued. \"My objection is to how easily we could have overlooked it.\"\n\nWe drew two parallel columns. Public documents could receive a dated correction note that cited the original. Confidential statements would be reviewed with their contributor, with only an appropriately limited public summary when the contributor agreed. Emergency information could be used for safety without turning a frightened traveler's identity into festival gossip.\n\nA visitor from Tideglass arrived with a damaged invoice. They wanted to know whether the bill had been paid before the old closure, but they did not want their family's name displayed.\n\n\"Can you check without publishing it?\" they asked.\n\nNeri explained the process we'd just written.\n\n\"Good,\" the visitor said. \"Then I'd like you to look.\"\n\nThe transaction was small, one box of hinges. It did not name the missing keeper or resolve the Closed Bells. Orin recorded what it did establish and carefully marked the rest unknown.\n\nI felt a strange relief. The blank signature wasn't the only place where uncertainty mattered. Every small record needed a way for people to challenge it without surrendering the privacy the new rules were meant to protect.\n\nWhen the visitor left, Severus looked at Orin.\n\n\"You have improved the procedure.\"\n\nOrin smiled.\n\n\"By finding a mistake?\"\n\n\"That is generally how improvement begins,\" he said.\n\nMaui, carrying the empty paper wrapping from the printer, leaned in.\n\n\"You need an expert in mistakes? Lucky day. I've made some legendary ones. None came with such spectacularly boring handwriting, but I can adapt.\"\n\n\"Please refrain,\" Severus answered.\n\nI laughed, and Neri added the correction policy to the first page where every reader could see it.\n\nThe ledger was opened to public review the following week.\n\nPeople came with questions, corrections, and memories. Some brought documents; others asked whether they could give testimony privately. One person declined to leave a name and was told that was their choice. Another requested a copy of the revised emergency rules for a homeworld council dealing with flood evacuations.\n\nNeri gave them the public version.\n\nNobody received keys to private files merely because they were curious.\n\nMaui came in during the second afternoon, carrying a parcel of paper from the printer. He set it on the table and looked at the ledger.\n\n\"A ledger without a column for the biggest hero in the room?\" he asked, setting down the parcel. \"I demand a recount.\"\n\n\"There isn't a contest,\" Neri said.\n\n\"Then why am I losing?\"\n\nMini Maui drew an enormous star on his chest, struck it out, and pointed to the unfilled private record. For a moment Maui's expression turned thoughtful.\n\n\"People used to write down who belonged to whom?\" he asked.\n\n\"Who had authority to send travelers away or keep them here,\" Neri said.\n\nHis jaw tightened. He could joke about a competition, but the idea of somebody being trapped by a mark in a book seemed to anger him in a way the missing line never had.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, a little too loudly. \"New rule. If somebody wants to go, you let them go. If they want to come back, you ask whether they want company. If they want bread—\"\n\n\"Mara handles bread,\" I interrupted.\n\n\"She does. Very well. The great Maui merely delivers it.\"\n\nHe grinned again, but he didn't try to fill the blank line. It wasn't his story to complete.\n\n\"That's the famous blank line?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" I said.\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"Looks smaller than I imagined.\"\n\n\"Most important things do.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nSeverus made a disapproving noise.\n\n\"Please do not encourage him.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nLater, when the visitors had gone, I found Neri standing alone by the display case.\n\n\"How are you?\" I asked.\n\nThey considered.\n\n\"Less certain than I once was. More responsible, perhaps.\"\n\n\"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nThey smiled faintly.\n\n\"I thought telling the truth would finally let me stop carrying it.\"\n\n\"Did it?\"\n\n\"No. But now other people can help carry the work.\"\n\nI looked at the new ledger.\n\nThe old book would remain in the archive, preserved with its flaws. The new one would be revised when evidence changed or policies failed. Its pages were numbered, its handling rules public, and its blank lines explained instead of quietly filled by whoever held the pen.\n\nMaui waited for me at the arch.\n\n\"Ready to go home?\" he asked.\n\n\"In a minute.\"\n\nI wrote one final note in the green notebook.\n\n*Accountability is not a perfect story. It is a record that lets other people ask questions tomorrow.*\n\nI closed it.\n\nThen I took Maui's hand and went through the open passage toward the square, where the lanterns were coming on one by one.\n\nThe next day a young visitor came back with a correction.\n\nTheir family's crossing had been entered as a departure at dusk, but the ferry hadn't sailed until the following morning. The public map used that time to estimate the last safe crossing, and the error made the old route look more reliable than it was.\n\nOrin found the source note.\n\n\"You're right,\" they said. \"The time was copied from the departure request, not the actual tide record.\"\n\nNeri asked whether the visitor wanted to attach a private statement or have the correction attributed anonymously.\n\n\"Anonymous, please.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nThe public page received a dated correction and a link to the weather record. Nobody wrote the visitor's name beside it. The next ferry schedule carried the revised time.\n\nI watched the process with a feeling I couldn't quite name. We had spent so much time worrying about a missing historical signature that I'd forgotten how much a smaller error could matter to somebody trying to get home.\n\nSeverus came to inspect the update. He checked the reference, nodded once, and said, \"A better ledger is one that can be corrected without a trial.\"\n\nOrin grinned.\n\nMaui leaned over my shoulder.\n\n\"And one that doesn't expect me to understand every column?\"\n\n\"That may be asking too much,\" I said.\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThere were ordinary lives on both sides of every line we kept. That was what the new book had to remember.\n\nThe ledger stayed where it belonged.\n\nNot beneath a crown.\n\nOn a table that could be reached.","word_count":1858}}};for(const story of lib.series){const chapters=updates[story.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of story.chapters){const x=chapters[ch.id];if(!x||x.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=x.content;ch.title=x.title;ch.word_count=x.word_count;}}})();
